nst `$self` to produce `https://example.com/api/shared/foo#/components/requestBodies/Foo`.\\ndocs/references/oas32/appendices/f-base-uri.md:56:The portion of that URI before the `#` matches the `$self` of the second document, so the reference target is resolved to `#/components/requestBodies/Foo` in that second document.\\ndocs/references/oas32/appendices/f-base-uri.md:59:This matches the `$id` at `#/components/schemas/Foo/$id` so it points to that Schema Object.\\ndocs/references/oas32/appendices/f-base-uri.md:60:That Schema Object has a subschema with `$ref: bar`, which is resolved against the `$id` to produce `https://example.com/api/schemas/bar`, which matches the `$id` at `#/components/schemas/Bar/$id`.\\ndocs/references/oas32/appendices/f-base-uri.md:65:Note also that it is impossible for the reference at `#/components/schemas/Foo/properties/bar/$ref` to reference the schema at `#/components/schemas/Bar` using _only_ a JSON Pointer fragment, as the JSON Pointer would be resolved relative to `https://example.com/api/schemas/foo`, not to the OpenAPI document's base URI from `$self`.\\ndocs/references/oas32/appendices/f-base-uri.md:88:components:\\ndocs/references/oas32/appendices/f-base-uri.md:94:            $ref: \\\"#/components/api/schemas/Foo\\\"\\ndocs/references/oas32/appendices/f-base-uri.md:123:Since the Schema Object at `#/components/schemas/Foo` does not contain an `$id`, the reference in its subschema uses the OpenAPI document's base URI, which is taken from the `Content-Location` header of its part within the `multipart/related` format.\\ndocs/references/oas32/appendices/f-base-uri.md:124:The resulting reference to `https://example.com/schemas/bar` matches the `Content-Location` header of the second part, which according to RFC2557 allows the reference target to be located within the multipart archive.\\ndocs/references/oas32/appendices/f-base-uri.md:139:components:\\ndocs/references/oas32/appendices/f-base-uri.md:185:        $ref: \\\"shared/foo#/co